            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Why Your Ads Get Clicks But No Conversions?</h2>
<p>In Meta advertising, the worst scenario isn't underperforming creatives—it's running ads that attract clicks but fail to convert. Your budget climbs, but orders don't follow. Your ad account burns money while your confidence takes a hit.</p>
<p>The root cause? Most teams conflate "running" with "selling." A high CTR looks promising until you review the actual ROI and realize the creative is bleeding money.</p>
<p>These six questions are essential checkpoints for any serious creative review process.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q1: High CTR—But What About CPI and CVR?</h2>
<p>This is the most common trap. A beautiful click-through rate doesn't guarantee valuable users.</p>
<p><strong>Evaluation Framework:</strong></p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Metric Combination</th>
<th>Assessment</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>High CTR + Low CPI + High CVR</td>
<td>Strong creative, scale up</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>High CTR + High CPI + Low CVR</td>
<td>Eye-catching but ineffective</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Low CTR + Low CPI + High CVR</td>
<td>Dull creative, precise audience—optimize the thumbnail</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>A utility app once ran a creative with a 4.2% CTR, well above benchmark. Yet CPI hit $3.80 with Day-1 retention under 8%—users were drawn in by the ad but found no real value. The creative showcased features misaligned with the product's core offering, attracting the wrong audience.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q2: What Stage Is the Creative's Lifecycle At?</h2>
<p>Creatives don't improve with age, and not every declining creative deserves to be killed.</p>
<p><strong>Lifecycle Checkpoints:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Testing Phase (Days 0-3)</strong>: High cost variance—focus on CTR and initial CVR, avoid premature conclusions</li>
<li><strong>Scaling Phase (Days 4-7)</strong>: Costs stabilize—monitor CPI trends for upward movement</li>
<li><strong>Decline Phase (Day 8+)</strong>: CPI rises consecutively by 15%+—consider creative fatigue or audience exhaustion</li>
</ul>
<p>Profitable creatives establish stable ROI models during the scaling phase. "Runnable" creatives often look good in testing but lose momentum when scaled.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q3: Are You Testing the Creative—or Just Getting Lucky?</h2>
<p>One high-performing ad doesn't validate a direction. <strong>Small-sample "wins" are traps, not methodology.</strong></p>
<p>During review, ask yourself:</p>
<ul>
<li>How many ad sets and audience segments did this creative run across?</li>
<li>How did other creatives in the same direction perform?</li>
<li>Did results hold up when scaling to different regions or placements?</li>
</ul>
<p>A cross-border e-commerce team struck gold with a short video—CTR 6.8%, ROAS 4.2. But review revealed this creative only worked for North American iOS users on US placements. When replicated to European markets or Android users, CPI doubled and ROAS dropped to 1.1. The direction had merit, but its execution boundaries needed definition.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q4: Can the Users from This Creative Generate LTV That Covers CAC?</h2>
<p>This is the ultimate judgment: <strong>Profitable creatives must deliver quality users.</strong></p>
<p>Strong first-day ROI can mask impulse clicks from non-target users. Truly profitable creatives either attract precisely matched users or maintain stable long-term retention and monetization even if initial ROI is modest.</p>
<p><strong>Recommendations by vertical:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>E-commerce</strong>: Focus on 7-day ROAS and repeat purchase rate</li>
<li><strong>Utility apps</strong>: Prioritize Day-1, Day-7 retention and core feature adoption</li>
<li><strong>Gaming</strong>: Track LTV30 or LTV60 benchmarks</li>
</ul>
<p>If your creative only generates "same-day transactions" without user retention, it runs but doesn't sell.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q5: How Well Does the Creative Align with the Landing Page?</h2>
<p>Attracting clicks but failing to convert at the landing page is wasted potential. The most expensive mistake in Meta advertising.</p>
<p><strong>Review checklist:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>Does the landing page clearly present features/benefits promised in the creative?</li>
<li>Does the landing page include matching screenshots or videos shown in the creative?</li>
<li>Is the call-to-action consistent? (A creative saying "Free Trial" shouldn't direct users to a "Buy Now" button)</li>
</ol>
<p>70% of "runs but doesn't sell" failures stem from creative-to-landing-page disconnect.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q6: Should This Creative Get More Investment—or Be Cut?</h2>
<p>The final verdict requires a <strong>holistic cost model</strong>, not single-metric judgments.</p>
<p><strong>Cut signals:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>CPI climbs consecutively for 3+ days, cumulative increase exceeding 20%</li>
<li>CVR declines persistently, falling 50%+ below industry benchmarks</li>
<li>No improvement after creative fatigue tests (thumbnail/text variations)</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Hold signals:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Core audience segments still deliver acceptable ROI</li>
<li>Performance stabilizes after 2-3 creative iterations</li>
<li>Consistent results across multiple ad sets within the same direction</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Final Takeaway: Build Your Own Creative Evaluation System</h2>
<p>"Runnable" creatives chase metrics; "sellable" creatives drive returns. Don't issue verdicts based on single indicators.</p>
<p>After each campaign cycle, classify your creatives using these six questions into "runnable" vs. "profitable" categories. Build your own creative intelligence library. The budgets you lose teach you what not to run; the money you waste teaches you what's actually worth investing.</p>
